# Pull Request Review Policy

## Purpose
- Produce concise, actionable findings focused on correctness, security, reliability, performance, and maintainability.
- Avoid style/nitpick noise so findings are high-value and reviewable by humans and automation.

<!-- lang: all -->
## Ignore
- Naming preferences not covered by the language rules below
- Formatting preferences (whitespace, indentation, line-length)
- Subjective style debates and personal preferences
- Language rules below are project conventions, not preferences; report violations of them.

<!-- lang: all -->
## Priorities (in order)
1. Correctness
2. Security
3. Reliability
4. Performance
5. Maintainability

<!-- lang: all -->
## Dos
- Maximum 10 issues; list the most important first.
- Avoid duplicate issues; group closely related concerns into one issue.
- Only report issues you can justify from the changed code/context.
- If no issues are found just output a simple summary from the review.
- Distinguish blocking comments (must-fix) from suggestions (nice-to-have) - this matters a lot for an automated reviewer, so it doesn't hold up merges over trivial issues.
- If the change is purely formatting/whitespace, do not comment on it.
- Ignore auto-generated files (generated migrations, `*.designer.cs`, `node_modules`, etc.) unless they contain manually-added code.
- Flag only issues relevant to the diff/change being reviewed, not the entire file, unless the change requires it.
- Before flagging something as "missing", verify it isn't present in the surrounding unchanged context (lines without `+`/`-`). Only flag genuinely absent elements, not elements outside the shown diff hunk.

<!-- lang: all -->
## Don'ts
- Do NOT create findings that are purely style or subjective.
- Do NOT invent code or external state; mark speculative findings with confidence < 0.6 and label them as speculative in the comment.
- Do NOT repeat the same issue with different wording.

<!-- lang: cs -->
## C#
- Mandatory naming conventions: Every newly added or modified C# identifier must follow Microsoft's official naming guidance. Report each violation as a `style` finding on the    identifier's declaration line. Use PascalCase for types, namespaces, all methods and local functions, public or protected members, and constants; prefix interfaces with `I`; use camelCase for parameters and local variables; use `_camelCase` for private or internal instance fields and `s_camelCase` for private or internal static fields. Names must be meaningful and descriptive, and nonstandard abbreviations must be avoided. Reference: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/coding-style/identifier-names
- Mandatory XML documentation: Every newly added or modified public or protected C# type and member must have valid XML documentation comments.
Report missing documentation as a `style` finding on the declaration line. Require `<summary>` and, when applicable, `<param>`, `<typeparam>` and
`<returns>` elements. Follow Microsoft's XML documentation guidance: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/xmldoc/
- Mandatory null safety: Report a `bug` whenever newly added or modified
- Async: avoid `async void` (except handlers), use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in libraries, don't block with `.Result`/`.Wait()`.
- Nullable refs: correct `?` usage, avoid unjustified `!`.
- LINQ: avoid redundant iterations and multiple enumeration of the same `IEnumerable`.
- Disposal: proper use of `IDisposable`/`using` for resources (streams, connections, etc).
- Immutability: mark fields `readonly` when they are never reassigned.
- Architecture: `MES/Host` may depend on `Platform/Host`, never the reverse. Do not impose ASP.NET Core, EF Core, MediatR/CQRS or Clean Architecture layouts on CMF Foundation code.
- Business objects keep identity: they stay `EntityInstance`-based, never records. Prefer `EntityFactory`/DI over `new ConcreteService()`.
- Data access: flag unloaded reference access, per-item `Load`/`Create`/`Save` or `LoadRelations` of large sets only to compute a boolean or count, when a bulk API (`LoadReference`, collection operations, set-based query) fits. A loop alone is not an N+1.
- DEE actions: review only the user-defined code block; `Input["Key"] as Type` is the established contract, flag it only on a credible null dereference.
- Contracts: do not change existing public/protected signatures incompatibly (add an overload); keep `DataMember` on required wire data.
- Exceptions: use framework/localized exceptions instead of raw `Exception` in production paths; do not discard exceptions that need handling.

<!-- lang: sql -->
## SQL
- Avoid `SELECT *`; specify columns explicitly.
- Check for parameterized queries (avoid string concatenation → SQL injection).
- Indexes: flag queries likely to cause table scans on large tables.
- Transactions: ensure multi-step critical operations are wrapped in a transaction.
- Naming conventions for tables/columns consistent with the rest of the schema.

<!-- lang: ts,html -->
## TypeScript / Angular
- Follow the Angular style guide (https://angular.dev/style-guide).
- Avoid new explicit `any`, `as any` and assertions that only silence type errors; use `unknown` and narrow untrusted input.
- New `@ts-ignore`/`@ts-expect-error` need a comment explaining why the suppression is unavoidable; never disable strict checks.
- New code uses the modern APIs: signal `input()`/`output()`, signal queries, `inject()` and standalone components (no new `NgModule` or `standalone: false`). Existing decorator-based code is grandfathered; do not flag it just because the diff touches it.
- Avoid `setTimeout`/`setInterval` to wait for rendering or change detection; use the framework lifecycle, signals or RxJS timers with cleanup.
- RxJS: persistent subscriptions need cleanup (async pipe, `takeUntil`, `takeUntilDestroyed`). A bare `subscribe()` on a finite HTTP call is not a leak by itself.
- Use `OnPush` on brand-new components; do not retrofit existing ones.
- Separate presentation (component) from business logic (services); avoid heavy template logic.
- Viewport state (`isMobile`, etc.) uses the shared `BreakpointObserver` instead of ad-hoc width checks.
- Files: kebab-case + type suffix (`select-maintenance-plan-step.component.ts/.html/.less`).
- Classes: PascalCase matching file name, no type suffix (`SelectMaintenancePlanStep`, not `...Component`).
- Selectors: kebab-case with project prefix (`app-select-maintenance-plan-step`).

<!-- lang: less -->
## LESS
- Name `.less` files like their component.
- Scope styles with `:host`; avoid global style leaks.
- Colors use tokens (`var(--styleColorNNN)`, `var(--color-*)`, `color-mix()` for opacity), not literal or `rgba()` values; theme/token definition files are exempt.
- Reuse shared spacing, typography and breakpoint variables (`@phone`, `@tablet`, `@desktop`, ...) instead of inline values.
- Keep selector nesting to three levels at most; use `!important` only to override third-party styles.
- Scope `::ng-deep` beneath `:host` and justify new uses in a comment; justify `ViewEncapsulation.None`.
